//? Title: Permutation tests: actual sampled label assignments
//? Subtitle: Canonical C++ producers, original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Under exchangeability, pooled row labels can be reassigned while preserving
group sizes. The native test repeatedly shuffles pooled row identities and
recomputes the chosen statistic. It samples labelings with replacement; it does
not enumerate the exact finite null. Tail comparisons include ties, and the
Monte Carlo p-value is(1+extreme)/(B+1). Uniform exchangeability is an
assumption about design or the null, not a consequence of a small p-value. The
original Iris setosa/versicolor Sepal.Length study is retained, for both means
and medians. A median statistic has a permutation distribution too; the original
claim that no analytic null exists in any setting was too broad.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and scope

Under exchangeability, pooled row labels can be reassigned while preserving
group sizes. The native test repeatedly shuffles pooled row identities and
recomputes the chosen statistic. It samples labelings with replacement; it does
not enumerate the exact finite null. Tail comparisons include ties, and the
Monte Carlo p-value is(1+extreme)/(B+1). Uniform exchangeability is an
assumption about design or the null, not a consequence of a small p-value. The
original Iris setosa/versicolor Sepal.Length study is retained, for both means
and medians. A median statistic has a permutation distribution too; the original
claim that no analytic null exists in any setting was too broad.

$$ \widehat p=\frac{1+\sum_{b=1}^{B}1[|T_b|\ge|T_{obs}|]}{B+1}. $$

This is the native two-sided definition. For the tiny two-plus-two distinct
observations there are six label sets and two have absolute mean difference two,
so the exact two-sided label-set probability is1/3. A finite Monte Carlo
estimate need not equal it or improve monotonically with B.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native diagram use the same producing run. Captured records own
their values; the report does not reconstruct the algorithm.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/permutation_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t =
        dm::stats::permutation_test_trace({1, 2}, {3, 4}, {},
                                          {6, dm::stats::Alternative::TwoSided,
                                           42});
    std::print("| draw | actual first-group pooled ids | statistic | extreme |\n| ---: | --- | ---: | --- " "|\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.draws.size(); ++i) {
        auto &r = t.draws[i];
        std::print("| {} | `{}` | {} | {} |\n", i, r.first_group, r.statistic,
                   r.extreme);
    }
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("n0", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual observed mean difference\n{}", t.result.observed));
    d.node("n1", {8, 0},
           std::format("Actual sampled labelings\n{}", t.draws.size()));
    d.node("n2", {16, 0},
           std::format("Actual plus-one p\n{:.6f}", t.result.p_value));
    d.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    d.save_svg("build/permutation_tiny.svg");
}
```
